WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.027 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A shopper buys tomato soup. Imagine

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:06.669 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that the receipt says chicken noodle.
Did the camera miss the flavor?

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.370 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Amazon's Just Walk Out uses cameras

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.381 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and shelf sensors to follow
the items shoppers take.

00:00:12.781 --> 00:00:17.879 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Researchers can study a shopping trip
inside a simulated store, like Sari Sandbox.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:23.221 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Virtual try-on shows shoppers how a pair
of glasses would look on their own face.

00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:27.094 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We studied a checkout model
that recognized the soup brand

00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:28.784 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and still chose the wrong flavor.

00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:34.364 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We present A Transferable Variant Signal
That Grocery Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:37.832 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
One wrong flavor leaves
us with three questions.

00:00:38.452 --> 00:00:41.321 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
First, did the camera model record the flavor,

00:00:41.321 --> 00:00:43.359 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
even though it chose the wrong description?

00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:45.844 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Second, can a distinction learned

00:00:45.844 --> 00:00:49.269 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from one brand help identify a
product from a different brand?

00:00:49.889 --> 00:00:54.540 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Third, how much does that help when the
only choices are flavors of the same brand?

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.979 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Look at this can. It is Campbell's Creamy
Tomato. The other can is Chicken Noodle.

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:04.793 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A vision encoder turns a
photo into a set of numbers.

00:01:04.793 --> 00:01:07.440 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These measurements are called image features.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.748 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A text encoder measures
each catalog description.

00:01:10.748 --> 00:01:14.503 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model ranks the descriptions by
how closely the measurements match.

00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:18.296 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For the tomato soup photo,
Chicken Noodle ranked first.

00:01:18.296 --> 00:01:21.048 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model got the brand
right and the flavor wrong.

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:24.721 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An earlier grocery retrieval
study found the correct

00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:27.646 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
item was often among the first five choices,

00:01:27.646 --> 00:01:29.171 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
even when it did not rank first.

00:01:29.951 --> 00:01:34.292 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photo passes through a vision
encoder. We freeze its weights.

00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:35.440 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The snowflake means

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.519 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that the same photo will give the same
measurements throughout the experiment.

00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:43.020 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We train a simple classifier
to predict the flavor

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.543 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from those image features.
This is a linear probe.

00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:50.015 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Only its weights change.
It never reads the catalog.

00:01:50.415 --> 00:01:53.865 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We test the probe on other
photos of the training brands.

00:01:53.865 --> 00:01:56.868 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each variety counts equally within its brand.

00:01:56.868 --> 00:02:03.056 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original labels, it scores 97.1
percent. With flavor labels assigned at random

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:07.487 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
within each brand, it scores
about 35 percent, close to chance.

00:02:07.887 --> 00:02:12.447 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This result shows that the encoder
retained information about the variety.

00:02:12.447 --> 00:02:16.159 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Matching a photo to a description
can still give the wrong flavor.

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:20.176 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Could a distinction learned from
one brand help with another?

00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:23.205 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We began with brands for
which we had labeled photos.

00:02:23.825 --> 00:02:27.340 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For each variety, we averaged
its photo measurements.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.345 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The arrow runs from the brand
average to the variety average.

00:02:31.345 --> 00:02:34.097 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It shows how that variety
differs from its brand.

00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:38.019 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now imagine laying a ruler along those arrows.

00:02:38.019 --> 00:02:41.320 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Along some directions the
varieties spread apart.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.347 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We kept 64 directions that
captured the most variation.

00:02:45.347 --> 00:02:47.480 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Together they form the variant subspace.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.977 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The calculation is called
singular value decomposition.

00:02:51.977 --> 00:02:57.108 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The columns of V give the directions we
measure. Sigma gives their strengths.

00:02:57.108 --> 00:03:00.393 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
U shows how each variety
contributes to each direction.

00:03:01.173 --> 00:03:03.746 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To check whether the variety names mattered,

00:03:03.746 --> 00:03:06.123 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
we repeated the calculation with one change.

00:03:06.523 --> 00:03:09.916 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We shuffled the labels among
photos of the same brand.

00:03:09.916 --> 00:03:13.523 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A tomato photo could now
carry a chicken noodle label.

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:17.192 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photos stayed in place, and
each name appeared just as often.

00:03:17.592 --> 00:03:19.568 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We learned a second set of directions

00:03:19.568 --> 00:03:23.055 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from these shuffled labels.
The test labels stayed correct.

00:03:23.835 --> 00:03:28.277 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now we try the directions on three
coffee roasts. No Starbucks photo

00:03:28.277 --> 00:03:31.217 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
or description was used
to learn these directions.

00:03:31.617 --> 00:03:36.483 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We project each coffee photo and each
description onto the same directions.

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:39.577 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It is like measuring both
with the same set of rulers.

00:03:39.577 --> 00:03:43.140 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Using measurements learned from
other brands is subspace transfer.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.080 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These are two real photos of Sumatra.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.431 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original labels,
the first matches Sumatra.

00:03:49.431 --> 00:03:53.848 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The second matches Breakfast Blend.
The roast is the same in both photos.

00:03:54.248 --> 00:03:56.980 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each of these ten photos shows Sumatra.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.672 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original training labels,
nine out of ten were correct.

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:04.193 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With shuffled training labels,
zero out of ten were correct.

00:04:04.193 --> 00:04:06.796 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model predicted Pike
Place for every photo.

00:04:07.196 --> 00:04:10.312 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This was our clearest example
within a single brand.

00:04:10.312 --> 00:04:14.301 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To see how often the distinction
transfers, we need more than one roast.

00:04:15.081 --> 00:04:18.256 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We repeat the experiment ten times. Each time,

00:04:18.256 --> 00:04:21.825 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
we train on one group of brands
and test on a different group.

00:04:21.825 --> 00:04:23.555 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each row is one model.

00:04:23.555 --> 00:04:27.843 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The numbers show how often the correct
description ranked first for the new brands.

00:04:28.463 --> 00:04:31.748 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Read the first row. With 64 directions,

00:04:31.748 --> 00:04:36.223 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
SigLIP2 gets about 52 percent
correct using the original labels

00:04:36.223 --> 00:04:39.132 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and 38 percent using shuffled labels.

00:04:39.132 --> 00:04:41.608 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
All nine models improve on average.

00:04:41.608 --> 00:04:45.256 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Using the full representation
still gives higher accuracy.

00:04:45.876 --> 00:04:50.741 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now take the same test photos and remove
every other brand from the catalog.

00:04:50.741 --> 00:04:55.307 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For a coffee photo, the choices are now
only the roasts of that coffee brand.

00:04:55.927 --> 00:05:01.867 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2, the gain over shuffled
labels drops from 13.6 percentage points

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:07.151 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to 3.9. For MobileCLIP,
it drops from 4.5 to 1.2.

00:05:07.151 --> 00:05:11.355 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On both models, most of the advantage
came from distinguishing brands.

00:05:11.355 --> 00:05:14.384 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Telling varieties of the
same brand apart was harder.

00:05:15.164 --> 00:05:17.148 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Let us return to the three questions.

00:05:17.768 --> 00:05:23.293 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Did the encoder record the flavor? Yes. A
simple classifier could read the variety

00:05:23.293 --> 00:05:26.347 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from a photo even when
matching it to text failed.

00:05:26.967 --> 00:05:32.843 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Did the directions transfer? Yes. All nine
models ranked new products more accurately

00:05:32.843 --> 00:05:36.996 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with the original training labels
than with shuffled labels, on average.

00:05:37.616 --> 00:05:42.087 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
How much did that help within a brand?
On the two models we tested this way,

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:46.705 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
only about a third of the gain remained when
every choice was from the correct brand.

00:05:48.085 --> 00:05:52.591 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A receipt needs the exact variety.
To evaluate a checkout model,

00:05:52.591 --> 00:05:55.307 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
give it several flavors of the same brand.

00:05:55.307 --> 00:05:58.613 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
That test reveals errors a
whole-catalog score can hide.

00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:03.708 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For representation learning, a wrong
answer leaves two possibilities.

00:06:03.708 --> 00:06:05.856 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The encoder may have missed the difference,

00:06:05.856 --> 00:06:08.588 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
or the comparison with
text may have missed it.

00:06:08.588 --> 00:06:13.193 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Testing the image on its own lets
us distinguish them. In this study,

00:06:13.193 --> 00:06:17.159 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
the encoder retained a distinction
that catalog matching often missed.

00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:20.909 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The coffee photographs
illustrate why this matters.

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:23.232 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Changing how we compare the same photos

00:06:23.232 --> 00:06:26.243 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and descriptions changes
which roast ranks first.

00:06:26.863 --> 00:06:28.164 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Thank you for listening.
